common_passwords.txt — offline common/breached-password screening corpus
=========================================================================

Source : SecLists — Passwords/Common-Credentials
         https://github.com/danielmiessler/SecLists
         Two members of the Pwdb series. The generated block at the end of this file
         names both, in the order they are concatenated, and records what each one
         contributes.
License: MIT (SecLists is MIT-licensed; the underlying lists aggregate publicly
         disclosed breach corpora). Redistributed here under that license.

Contents: one entry per line, used by messagefoundry.auth.policy as an offline
membership check (ASVS 6.2.4/6.2.12) so a local password that is a known-common or
breached value is rejected. No network call (no live HIBP lookup); a fuller
k-anonymity (SHA-1 prefix) corpus is a planned follow-up. The file is data, never
executed.

THE LIVE COUNTS AND THE DIGEST LIVE IN THE GENERATED BLOCK AT THE END OF THIS FILE.
`scripts/security/build_password_corpus.py` recomputes them from the shipped corpus,
and `tests/test_auth_core.py` fails when the block stops describing the file. Before
BACKLOG #1433 they were hand-copied measurements with no tool behind them, so a corpus
edit left the notice describing a file that no longer existed, and the only remedy on
offer was to re-derive each count by hand.

Numbers that appear in the PROSE below are the BACKLOG #1134 argument for why the
corpus is shaped this way, not measurements of the file as it stands. Where the two
could drift, the block is the record.

Why a SUBSET of the second file (BACKLOG #1134)
-----------------------------------------------
ASVS 6.2.4 asks for a check against "at least the top 3000 passwords WHICH MATCH
THE APPLICATION'S PASSWORD POLICY". At the shipped `password_min_length` almost none
of the original 10,000 entries reached that length, and only four of those sat in the
top 3,000 ranks — so the screen added next to nothing on top of the length rule. How
many of them clear the policy today is the block's "from run 1" column at the shipped
floor, measured rather than remembered.

An entry the policy would already reject on another clause can only reject what
that clause already rejects. So the second file was filtered through the real
`PasswordPolicy` object rather than a length check, with `check_breached` off
(it would otherwise reject candidates against the very corpus being extended)
and `check_username` off (a corpus entry has no user context).

Hygiene filter
--------------
Three candidate entries were dropped that the policy would otherwise have accepted, and
BOTH classes were found by this repository's commit gates rather than anticipated: one
carrying a C0 control byte (invisible in an editor, in `git diff` and in review), and two
shaped like routable IPv4 addresses. The leak gate's own message says a host string must be
REMOVED rather than allowlisted -- allowlisting would train a reviewer to wave through the
exact shape that gate exists to catch, to keep three worthless corpus entries. The rebuild
applies both classes using the gates' own definitions rather than a second copy of the rule.

Why the original 10,000 were KEPT rather than replaced
-------------------------------------------------------
At the shipped minimum they are all redundant — the length clause rejects them
first. But `password_min_length` is an OPERATOR SETTING. A site that lowers it
makes every short entry load-bearing again, so replacing the file would silently
delete their protection for exactly the configuration that needs it most. The
block's by-floor table is what that protection looks like at each floor.

Ordering
--------
Both runs are rank-ordered (most common first) within themselves, so the file is
two ordered runs rather than one. This does not affect behaviour: the loader
builds a `frozenset` and only membership is consulted.

Regenerating this file
----------------------
    python scripts/security/build_password_corpus.py --check   verify the block
    python scripts/security/build_password_corpus.py --write   regenerate the block

Rebuilding the corpus ITSELF needs a SecLists checkout, which this repository does not
vendor: pass `--seclists <checkout>` to either mode to rebuild it or to verify that the
shipped file still reproduces from the two upstream members.

